fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1130", description: some(value: "Proposal 056 — POSITIONAL variadic capture: refused at load, while THREE records claim three different things, none of them what happens. Split out of WI-1129 (which owns the rule-head rest-pattern face, section 2.3); this ticket owns the positional half and the diagnostic.\n\nMEASURED on the current tree (probes, since removed), with `cap[R](x: Int64, ...rest: R) -> R = rest`:\n  * `cap(1, a: 2, b: 3).a` -> Int(2). Named capture WORKS.\n  * `cap(a: 5).a` -> Int(5) with the capture as the only parameter. WORKS.\n  * `cap(1)` -> Int(1), the empty capture, the identity (OQ #6). WORKS.\n  * `cap(rest: 5)` -> Int(5). Naming the capture parameter EXPLICITLY binds it directly — the \"already-normalized / explicit-record guard\" path in normalize_variadic_capture.\n  * `cap(5)`, `cap(1, 2)`, `cap(1, 2, a: 3)` -> LOAD ERROR, every arrangement. A POSITIONAL leftover is refused.\n\nTHE THREE RECORDS, AND HOW EACH IS WRONG:\n  (1) 056 section 3 OQ #2: \"`...args: R` captures BOTH: a positional leftover lands in `R` as `_N` (section 4.5 named-tuple positional sugar), so one mechanism covers named and positional — no separate positional-variadic feature.\" FALSE: it lands nowhere; it is refused.\n  (2) 056 section 5 (Out of scope): \"A SEPARATE positional-variadic feature — not needed, and not built. Positional capture is ALREADY SUBSUMED.\" FALSE in the load-bearing half: it is not subsumed, it is rejected, so the feature is neither built nor subsumed.\n  (3) 056's status line and normalize_variadic_capture's own doc comment (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs): \"a positional leftover BINDS THE CAPTURE PARAMETER DIRECTLY (positional capture is a deferred follow-up).\" FALSE as written — what binds directly is an argument that NAMES the capture parameter (`cap(rest: 5)`), which is a different spelling and a different code path. A positional one errors.\nSo the proposal body says it works, the status line says something else happens, and the implementation does neither. Note the shape: (1) and (2) over-claim, (3) under-claims but still misdescribes, and all three read as settled.\n\nTHE DIAGNOSTIC IS ALSO WRONG — the WI-757 class. `cap(5)` produces TWO errors, both blaming a parameter the author never wrote:\n    type mismatch in cap.rest (op-arg): expected Int64, got ()\n    type mismatch in cap.rest (op-arg): expected a named argument matching a distinct unbound parameter, got named argument 'rest' binds a parameter already given\nThe author wrote `cap(5)`. The capture normalization appended its own synthesized `rest: ()` and then the positional argument collided with it, so the message describes the REWRITE rather than the source. This is the same defect WI-757 fixed for the row-lambda macros: the synthesized form's error speaking instead of one about the text.\n\nFIRST DECISION, and it is a decision and not a lookup: implement positional capture as OQ #2 describes (a positional leftover becomes an `_N` field of the record, which section 4.5's positional sugar already gives a meaning), or REFUSE it deliberately. Both are defensible — the `_N` reading makes one mechanism cover both spellings as the proposal intends, while refusing keeps the capture purely name-keyed and avoids a record whose components are half named and half synthetic. What is NOT defensible is the present state, where three documents disagree and the error blames the wrong text.\n\nSCOPE: (a) make the behaviour and the records agree — whichever way it is decided, correct 056 section 3 OQ #2, section 5, the status line, and normalize_variadic_capture's doc comment, since a reader consults whichever is nearest; (b) fix the diagnostic so it names the ARGUMENT the author wrote and why it cannot be placed, with the synthesized capture record never appearing in a user-facing message; (c) if implementing, the `_N` labels must not collide with a named leftover also called `_N` — the WI-805 distinctness rule applies to the captured record and is enforced in normalize_variadic_capture.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the decided behaviour is DRIVEN — a positional leftover either reaches the body as the `_N` component (assert the VALUE, not that it loads) or is refused by a diagnostic naming the author's own argument; a control shows the named path and `cap(rest: 5)` unchanged; all four records agree with the measured behaviour and cite each other; existing wi727_fix and wi1127_condition_param tests green (both ride the named capture); a back-out measurement is stated at the test site; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test"), ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-727"]), status: Open)
